%[text] %[text:anchor:T_9237] # Greenhouse Model 
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](file:../MainMenu.m)
%[text] In this script, you will design the control algorithm for a greenhouse. The system relies on a fan blowing fresh air from the outside to cool down the inside of the greenhouse during the warmer months of the year. 
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:3823)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_51183E7B] **Before you get started:**
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_BF646A47] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**.  Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![Live script hide code icon](text:image:9427) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:04ba) Although the code is hidden, some interactivity requires familiarity with MATLAB. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2-hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_8F8A032D] ![Warning symbol](text:image:4ae3)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one. Some sections depend on variables created in prior sections and will generate errors if run out of order.
%[text] The ![Try this icon](text:image:8b10)  and  ![Exercise icon](text:image:5556) icons refer to two different types of interactive activities that you will find in this script. The ![Try this icon](text:image:7d30)  usually indicates an interaction where you will explore the visualization of some concept introduced in this script. The ![Exercise icon](text:image:55e6) interactions are designed to challenge your understanding of those concepts and may be used for grading and completion checks by your professor.
%[text] Additional
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4925] ## Introduction
%[text] In [GreenhouseControl.m](file:GreenhouseControl.m), we developed the control algorithm for the greenhouse's fan. We could build a greenhouse prototype and verify that the fan effectively cools the greenhouse during the hottest summer days. However, building a prototype is expensive, and it would take days, weeks, or months to verify the system's efficiency. Therefore, we have decided to develop a model based design approach to study the ventilation's impact on the greenhouse's temperature.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_61c4] ## Model based design using Simscape
%[text] Simscape is a powerful physical modeling and simulation tool. It allows engineers to represent and simulate complex physical systems using a block diagram approach. Simscape enables the integration of physical simulation (electrical, mechanical, hydraulic, and other) into Simulink, providing a comprehensive platform for designing and analyzing dynamic systems.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5fcd] ### Building a Simscape model
%[text] In this script, we provide a Simscape model to simulate the temperature inside the greenhouse over a full year of data. The model relies on ambient air temperature and solar flux data gathered at a weather station in Natick, MA.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:0041) **Try.** Open greenhouse' Simscape thermal model.
open(fullfile("Models","GreenhousePlant.slx"))
  %[control:button:33b4]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] In the thermal model, the **Inside (Thermal Mass)** block represents the air's thermal mass inside the greenhouse. Its parameters include the total air mass in the greenhouse and the specific heat.
%[text] We detected an error in the formula for the total air mass contained in the greenhouse. The total mass should be defined as:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $M\_{t} = \\rho V${"altText":"M\_{t} = \rho V"}
%[text]{"align":"center"} $V= HL^2${"altText":"V= HL^2"}
%[text] where $M\_t${"altText":"M\_t"} is the total mass, $\\rho${"altText":"\rho"} is the density (`Density)`, $V${"altText":"V"} is the greenhouse's volume, $H${"altText":"H"} is the greenhouse's height (height`)`, and $L${"altText":"L"} is the greenhouse's length (`Length`). The model workspace has been prepopulated with some parameters including:
%[text] - `Density` - the air density
%[text] - `Height` - the greenhouse's height
%[text] - `Length` - the greenhouse's length \
%[text] Because these variable are declared in the model workspace, you can't see them in your MATLAB workspace, but they can be used in the model's blocks.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3432) **Exercise.** Double-click on the Inside block and correct the formula entered in the mass parameter.
  %[control:button:9b68]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckThermalMass
%%
%[text] Now that the model is set correctly, you can simulate over a full year of data and visualize the temperature inside the greenhouse.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:6a73) **Try.** Simulate over a full year of data.
  %[control:button:11e9]{"position":[1,2]}
try
    SimOut = sim(fullfile("Models","GreenhousePlant.slx"));
    clear SimOut
catch ErrorMessage
    warning("The simulation could not run.")
    warning("This is because Simscape require a special solver attached to the physical domain (colored line).")
end
%%
%[text] The Simscape solver is already included in the model and must only be connected to the physical domain to fix the error message encountered in the running stage.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3b3d) **Exercise.** Add a connection between the solver and the physical domain (colored line).
  %[control:button:6c84]{"position":[1,2]}
isReady = CheckSolver;
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:26ec) **Try.** Simulate over a full year of data.
  %[control:button:638c]{"position":[1,2]}
if isReady
    try
        tic
        SimOut = sim(fullfile("Models","GreenhousePlant.slx"));
        t = toc;
        disp("Good job! You simulated a full year in "+t+" seconds.")
        isReady = true;
    catch ErrorMessage
        warning("The simulation could not run.")
        warning("This is because Simscape require a special solver attached to the physical domain (colored line).")
    end
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4a02] ### Exploring Results
%[text] Now that you have managed to simulate the full year of data, we should look at the results from the simulation. In this part of the script, we are going to explore three ways to check the simulation results:
%[text] 1. Using Simscape Results Explorer
%[text] 2. Using scopes
%[text] 3. Using MATLAB \
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_7e7e] #### Using Simscape Results Explorer
%[text] Simscape provides a Results Explorer app with detailed results from the physical domain. 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:040b) **Try.** Open Simscape Results Explorer using one of the following methods:
%[text] - In the model window, on the **Simulation** tab, click **Review Results** \> **Simscape Results Explorer**.
%[text] - Using the `sscexplore` function by clicking the following button \
  %[control:button:1798]{"position":[1,2]}
if exist("SimOut","var") && isReady
    sscexplore(SimOut.simlog)
else
    warning("Please complete the previous section in order before to run this part.")
end
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:42f3) **Exercise.** Plot the temperature of the greenhouse's air in **GreenhousePlant \> Inside \> T**, using the Simscape Results Explorer
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_858c] #### Using scopes
%[text] The second way to explore the results from the simulation is to use sensors and scope blocks. Sensor blocks measure physical quantities such as temperature in your physical domain, and scope blocks allow you to plot these quantities. The scope and sensor required to visualize the system's temperature have already been included in your model, but they are commented out.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:6313) **Exercise.**  Right-click on the block in the Sensor area to uncomment the block.
%[text] Tips: Uncomment the whole sensor area at once by selecting every block in the area.
  %[control:button:42d7]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckUncommentSensor
%%
%[text] The sensor is now included in the model but is not connected to the inside temperature signal.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:639d) **Exercise.** Connect the sensor to the **Inside** temperature
  %[control:button:0427]{"position":[1,2]}
if isReady
    load_system(fullfile("Models","GreenhousePlant"));
    InsideID = getSimulinkBlockHandle("GreenhousePlant/Inside");
    connectivities = get_param("GreenhousePlant/Temperature Sensor","PortConnectivity");
    isConnected = ~isempty(connectivities(1).DstBlock);
    isConnectedToInside = any(connectivities(1).DstBlock == InsideID);
    err = false;
    if ~isConnected
        err = true;
        warning("The sensor is not connected to the thermal model.")
    end
    if ~isConnectedToInside
        err = true;
        warning("The connection needs to be linked to the Inside block.")
    end
    if ~err
        disp("Good job! The sensor is connected to the thermal model correctly.")
    end
else
    warning("Please complete the previous section in order before to run this part.")
end
%[text] 
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:200a) **Try.** Double-click the scope and click the run button in the toolstrip.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_5c86] #### In MATLAB
%[text] You might have noticed that the signal going into the scope is being logged ![](text:image:37d4). This allows us to transfer the signal data from Simulink to MATLAB and analyze the results.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:426e) **Try.** Run the following code to plot the results.
  %[control:button:125e]{"position":[1,2]}
if isReady
    % Run the simulation
    SimOut = sim(fullfile("Models","GreenhousePlant"));
    
    % Retrieve the temperature
    SimOut = timeseries2timetable(SimOut.logsout{1}.Values);
    SimOut.Properties.VariableNames(1) = "Temperature (K)";
    
    % Set the date from January 1st 2022
    SimOut.Time = datetime("Jan 1, 2022") + SimOut.Time;
    
    % Plot temperature
    figure
    plot(SimOut,"Time","Temperature (K)")
else
    warning("Please complete the previous section in order before to run this part.")
end
%%
%[text] The temperature plot is not smooth because of the daily variation of the temperature. 
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:3183) **Exercise.** Retime the **SimOut** timetable with a **time step of 1 day** using a general rule **Maximum,** the output timetable should be **SimOut**.
TimeStep = "yearly"; %[control:dropdown:81fa]{"position":[12,20]}
Method = "nearest"; %[control:dropdown:2c3c]{"position":[10,19]}
  %[control:button:4c39]{"position":[1,2]}
if isReady
    SmoothSimOut = retime(SimOut,TimeStep,Method);
else
    warning("Please complete the previous section in order before to run this part.")
end

% Plot temperature
if exist("SmoothSimOut","var")
    figure
    plot(SmoothSimOut,"Time","Temperature (K)")
else
    warning("Please complete the previous section in order before to run this part.")
end
%%
%[text] The temperature output from the thermal model is in Kelvin. This might not be your preferred unit of temperature. You could convert the temperature from Kelvin to Fahrenheit and Celsius using the following relation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $T(^\\circ F) = a\_1 T(K) + b\_1 \\\\\nT(^\\circ F) = a\_2 T(K) + b\_2 \\\\${"altText":"T(^\circ F) = a\_1 T(K) + b\_1 \ T(^\circ F) = a\_2 T(K) + b\_2 \"}
%[text] A quick online search will give you the value for the coefficient $a\_{1,2}${"altText":"a\_{1,2}"} and $b\_{1,2}${"altText":"b\_{1,2}"}.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:4645) **Exercise.** Adjust the $a\_1${"altText":"a\_1"} and $b\_1${"altText":"b\_1"} values to create the Fahrenheit temperature from the Kelvin temperature.
if exist("SmoothSimOut","var")
    a = [ ]; %[control:editfield:64f1]{"position":[10,11]}
    b = [ ]; %[control:editfield:178f]{"position":[10,11]}
      %[control:button:5437]{"position":[5,6]}

    err = false;
    if isempty(a)
        warning("Please enter a value for a.")
        err = true;
    else
        if a < 1.81 && a > 1.79
        else
        warning("The value for a is wrong.")
        err = true;
        end
    end

    if isempty(b)
        warning("Please enter a value for a.")
        err = true;
    else
        if b < 461 && b > 458
        else
            warning("The value for b is wrong.")
            err = true;
        end
    end

    if ~err
        SmoothSimOut.("Temperature (F)") = a*SmoothSimOut.("Temperature (K)")+b;
    end

else
    warning("Please complete the previous section in order before to run this part.")
end
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:1a6b) **Exercise.** Adjust the $a\_2${"altText":"a\_2"} and $b\_2${"altText":"b\_2"} values to create the Celsius temperature from the Kelvin temperature. 
if exist("SmoothSimOut","var")
    a = [ ]; %[control:editfield:5fa4]{"position":[10,11]}
    b = [ ]; %[control:editfield:46f7]{"position":[10,11]}
      %[control:button:8207]{"position":[5,6]}

    err = false;
    if isempty(a)
        warning("Please enter a value for a.")
        err = true;
    elseif a == 1
    else
        warning("The value for a is wrong.")
        err = true;
    end

    if isempty(b)
        warning("Please enter a value for a.")
        err = true;
    elseif b < 274 && b > 273
    else
        warning("The value for b is wrong.")
        err = true;
    end

    if ~err
        SmoothSimOut.("Temperature (C)") = a*SmoothSimOut.("Temperature (K)")+b;
    end
else
    warning("Please complete the previous section in order before to run this part.")
end
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:08b5) **Try.** Plot the results in Fahrenheit
select = "Temperature (K)"; %[control:dropdown:7943]{"position":[10,27]}
if exist("SimOut","var") && isReady
    if any(string(SmoothSimOut.Properties.VariableNames) == select)
        figure
        plot(SmoothSimOut,"Time",select)
    else
        warning("The field "+select+" has not been create in the table yet. Rerun the previous interaction")
    end
else
    warning("Please complete the previous section in order before to run this part.")
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0743] ## Testing the design
%[text] At this point, we have implemented a thermal model to simulate the behavior of the greenhouse over a full year. It has a fan that constantly extracts 1 CFM (cubic feet per minute) of air from the greenhouse. In [GreenhouseControl.m](file:GreenhouseControl.m), we designed a control algorithm for the fan that depends on the current temperature in the greenhouse.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_0e9d] ### Adding the control algorithm
%[text] We prepare the same thermal model by adding the control algorithm from [GreenhouseControl.m](file:GreenhouseControl.m). 
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:8965) **Try.** Open the new model.
open(fullfile("Models","GreenhousePlantWithControl.slx"))
  %[control:button:5c21]{"position":[1,2]}
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:3ca0) **Try.** Run the simulation and observe the Stateflow Chart. It should indicate when the fan is on or off.
sim(fullfile("Models","GreenhousePlantWithControl.slx"));
  %[control:button:12d8]{"position":[1,2]}
%[text] 
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_257f] ### Testing multiple fan
%[text] We now have a complete model that can be used to study the effect of the fan on the greenhouse's temperature. We are now trying to define and determine the fan size necessary to maintain a temperature below 90F year long.
%[text] We are characterizing the size of the fan by its CFM value, the number of cubic feet per minute it can extract from the greenhouse.
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:4bb6) **Exercise.** Define the experiment plan by selecting the number of fans you would like to test, the minimum CFM, and the maximum CFM.
minCFM = 1; %[control:editfield:100a]{"position":[10,11]}
maxCFM = 1000; %[control:editfield:12fd]{"position":[10,14]}
numFan = 1; %[control:spinner:2b5f]{"position":[10,11]}
CFM = round(logspace(log10(minCFM),log10(maxCFM),numFan));
  %[control:button:9777]{"position":[1,2]}
CheckExperimentPlan(minCFM,maxCFM,numFan);
%%
%[text] Now that you have defined your experiment plan, we can use the same model used previously to test every fan.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:2702) **Try.** Click the **Run All Simulations** button to test every fans. Each line represents the temperature with a different fan; their CFM is included in the legend.
  %[control:button:99ed]{"position":[1,2]}
figure
for i = 1:numFan
    load_system(fullfile("Models","GreenhousePlantWithControl.slx"))
    set_param("GreenhousePlantWithControl/CFM","value",num2str(CFM(i)))
    SimOut = sim("GreenhousePlantWithControl");
    SimOut = FormatSimOut(SimOut);
    plot(SimOut,"Time","Temperature (K)",DisplayName=num2str(CFM(i))) %[control:dropdown:416f]{"position":[24,41]}
    hold on
end
hold off
legend(Location="bestoutside")
%%
%[text] Comparing the fans' performance over a full year is not evident. We are trying to control the temperature in the greenhouse over the warmest month of the year.
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:210f) **Try.** Use the drop-down list below to limit the data to the summer months.
startdate =  "January"; %[control:dropdown:3e56]{"position":[14,23]}
enddate =  "December"; %[control:dropdown:6ccc]{"position":[12,22]}
enddate  = datetime(enddate+" 1, 2022");
[y,m,d] = ymd(enddate);
xlim([datetime(startdate+" 1, 2022") datetime(y,m,eomday(y,m))])
%[text] ![](text:image:9fc0) **Reflect.** Why does the fan's size limit the temperature during the hottest day of the year?
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4c62] ## Conclusion
%[text] The cooling of the greenhouse is based on using a fan to bring cooler air from the outside of the greenhouse inside. This type of cooling will never be able to cool the greenhouse below the outdoor temperature. During the hottest days of the year, it might be more interesting to open the greenhouse and let the air flow freely instead of using electricity, which consumes electricity.
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_4fbb] ## Further exploration
%[text] In [ConnectectGreenhouse](file:ConnectedGreenhouse.m), you can learn how to connect your greenhouse to the internet to monitor the temperature. Moreover, the system can send an alert when the temperature exceeds a certain threshold and notify the user to open the greenhouse.
%[text] 
%[text] To learn more about thermal modeling, check out our [Thermodynamic](https://www.mathworks.com/matlabcentral/fileexchange/126784-thermodynamics?s_tid=srchtitle) module, where you can build your refrigerator.
